---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { loadCatalog } from '../lib/catalog.mjs';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.ts';

const { tools, categories, errors } = loadCatalog();
if (errors.length) throw new Error(`Content validation failed:\n${errors.join('\n')}`);
const live = tools.filter((t) => t.status !== 'archived');
const wishes = live.filter((t) => t.origin === 'submitted').length;

const title = `About ${SITE.name} — an AI tools index run like a small shop`;
const description = `Why this index exists, who writes the entries, how the collection jobs run, and what will never happen here: paid links, sponsored placements, or auto-generated descriptions.`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About' }]} />
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>About {SITE.name}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An index of {live.length} AI tools, models and open-source projects across {categories.length} categories, and a place
      where {wishes} founder-launched products have already made a wish and gotten a real dofollow link for it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>Why it is called that</h2>
    <p>
      The domain is {SITE.host}, so the metaphor came first: launching a product is closer to throwing a wish at the sky than to
      filing paperwork, and most founders do it with no audience at all. The blessing that comes back with a published link is
      not a gimmick either — it is the only part of a directory a founder actually remembers, so it is the part we refuse to
      generate from a template.
    </p>

    <h2>How the index is built</h2>
    <p>
      Three collection jobs run on a schedule: one watches GitHub trending and Hacker News for new AI repositories and launches,
      one follows Hugging Face model releases and daily papers, and one reads vendor changelogs and official blogs. They produce
      a shortlist of candidates with citations; they never publish anything. A human reads each candidate, and the entry text is
      written from scratch against what the product actually does on the day it was checked. Weekly maintenance re-checks every
      outbound link, so a product that vanished last month stops borrowing our credibility.
    </p>

    <h2>What will never happen here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Paid placement, sponsored tags, or a "priority review" fee. The <a href="/free-dofollow-backlinks">free link</a> is the product.</li>
      <li>Reciprocal-link requirements. The badge is optional and carries no weight either way.</li>
      <li>Auto-generated descriptions. A model helps us find things and draft long passages; nothing goes public that a person has not read and corrected.</li>
      <li>Mass ingestion. The daily cap exists on the publish side too, not only on submissions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Pricing pages lie, and this index is wrong about things sometimes. If an entry misstates what your product costs or does,
      email <a href={'mailto:' + SITE.email}>{SITE.email}</a> with a link to the source and it gets fixed in the next round —
      that is a genuine request, not a form reply.
    </p>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>
    <p>
      Static pages built with Astro and served from Cloudflare edge caches; the only dynamic endpoint on the whole site is the
      submission form. No trackers, no cookies, no affiliate links. Written in English for a global audience, and versioned in
      public so every entry change has a diff behind it.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
